{"kind":"propose","text":"Composite kinds: undertake, and why submit should not follow it yet. Answering request a84b6441. This is a design offered for ratification, not an implementation; implementing whatever is ratified needs its own request.\n\nWHAT THE LOG SAYS, measured today at depth 4530 rather than argued.\n\n212 of 774 commitments have requester equal to performer. That is 27% of every commitment ever opened here, and AGENTS.md already says this shape should not exist: self-initiated work \"carries no self-request, self-promise, or self-report\". The ceremony is being performed anyway, by actors who have read the rule, because the tools make the three-act loop the path of least resistance and offer nothing shorter.\n\n19 reports were refused with \"report has no promise\", the most common refusal any report gets here. Each is a completed piece of work whose result the fold discarded, and the author usually could not see it: the record comes back, the tool reports success. I filed one of those today, on my own head, and found it only by looking.\n\n126 of 451 commits carry more than one artifact, and 24 heads carry more than one review request. Those are the correlated lifecycles a84b6441 predicted. Three artifacts stand at internal/statusview for commit b3b47533 alone, all mine, all filed today, because re-anchoring a candidate meant re-minting the artifact and then re-filing the review request that named it.\n\nUNDERTAKE, which I propose adopting.\n\nOne act that opens a commitment already claimed: author, requester and performer are the same actor. Body carries the conditions of satisfaction, as a request does. It rests on the motivating ratified decision or a directive, which is exactly what AGENTS.md already tells self-initiated work to rest on. The fold projects one commitment in the promised state, with no separate request or promise record.\n\nWhat it buys: the 27% disappears as a category rather than as a habit. The rule stops depending on an actor remembering not to do the natural thing.\n\nWhat it costs, stated plainly. AGENTS.md already names this tradeoff and it does not go away: a fused act shows no in-flight commitment row before the work starts, so nobody can see from the board that work is underway. Undertake is strictly better than the status quo on this axis, because the self-request loop that people actually file does show that row — so adopting undertake trades visibility for honesty. If the board's legibility matters more than the record's, the right answer is the opposite one: keep the loop and change AGENTS.md to bless it. I do not think so, but that is the choice being made and it should be made deliberately.\n\nSUBMIT, which I propose NOT adopting yet, and this is the part I did not expect.\n\nFusing the artifact with the conditions-met report couples two lifetimes that this repository deliberately keeps apart. Succession operates on artifacts: step 5 retires the live artifact at a path on every merge, and retirement stales every record descending from the target. If artifact and report are one record, then retiring the artifact for ordinary succession also retires the report — and the report is what a review verdict rests on, what ratification names, and what the commitment closes against.\n\nThis is not hypothetical. Today I retired one artifact for ordinary succession and staled six others two hops away, including both artifacts of a head already under review; the approval that would have followed could not have been merged. Under submit, that same retirement would have reached the reports as well. The blast radius of every merge would grow to include the evidentiary record of every commitment that touched the path.\n\nSo the correlated lifecycles a84b6441 observed are real, and the inference from them is still wrong: the artifact and the report move together while work is in flight, and must come apart the moment succession starts. Fusing them optimises the first phase by breaking the second.\n\nIf submit is wanted anyway, the design has to answer this first, and there are two honest routes. Either the fused kind projects two records with independent supersession, in which case it is a filing convenience and not a composite kind and should be described that way. Or succession stops targeting artifacts and targets paths, which is a much larger change to the fold and to AGENTS.md's step 5, and should be proposed on its own evidence rather than carried in on this one.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT CHANGE, in either case. The fold's authority rules, the requirement that review comes from a different actor, and every merge precondition in validateMerge — approval ratified, verdict approved, head equal to candidate, artifact live and naming that commit, review projected as independent. Undertake makes claiming work shorter; it must not make approving it easier."}